    gmeow:catalogTargetMinimum 146 .

# ── residue-ratchet carve-out (gmeow:ResidueRatchetExemption) ─────────────────
#
# The projection-vocabulary residue ratchet
# (docs/PROJECTION-VOCABULARY-RATCHET.md §3) guards a vocabulary only when ONE
# grounding slice owns its authoring boundary. Two of the families above are
# general publication vocabularies with no such owner, so they carry no
# gmeow:ProjectionVocabulary and the correspondences onto them enter no residue
# count, no per-slice ceiling and no monotonicity ratchet.
#
# That was a documented absence with nothing counting it. These rows make it a
# registry commitment: each names the exempted family, states why it has no owner,
# and pins the number of shipped grounding correspondences riding the exemption.
# gmeow:exemptRowCeiling is LOWER-ONLY and the same family's
# gmeow:catalogTargetMinimum is RAISE-ONLY, so together they pin the carve-out
# from both sides — an added row and a deleted row are equally red. The mappings
# stage enforces both, plus the invariant that a family may never be exempt AND
# guarded (an exemption that outlives its reason reds instead of rotting).
#
# The forward path for both is the same and is deliberately NOT taken here:
# guarding either one reclassifies hundreds of pre-existing, legitimate alignment
# triples across dozens of (slice, vocabulary) cells as off-owner residue, each
# needing a new ceiling. That is a repo-wide ownership decision, not a ratchet
# tightening — so it is recorded, bounded, and left visible rather than done
# silently or forgotten.

gmeow:residueExemption-prov a gmeow:ResidueRatchetExemption ;
    rdfs:label "residue-ratchet exemption — PROV-O"@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:exemptCatalogFamily gmeow:catalogFamily-prov ;
    gmeow:exemptRationale "PROV-O is a general provenance-publication vocabulary that domain slices across the repository align to as ordinary by-reference correspondence records (Principle 5), so no one grounding slice is its authoring home. The kernel's logic:Plan / logic:Enactment rows onto it are grounding correspondences because their SUBJECTS are kernel terms — a fact about the source endpoint, which does not make logic: the catalog owner. Guarding it under logic: would reclassify the pre-existing prov: alignment triples authored across the domain slices as off-owner residue, each needing a new ceiling: a repo-wide ownership decision, not a ratchet tightening." ;
    gmeow:exemptRowCeiling 4 .

gmeow:residueExemption-schema-org a gmeow:ResidueRatchetExemption ;
    rdfs:label "residue-ratchet exemption — schema.org HowTo"@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:exemptCatalogFamily gmeow:catalogFamily-schema-org ;
    gmeow:exemptRationale "schema.org is a general web-publication vocabulary and the target of the shipped consumer down-projection profiles, aligned to by domain slices throughout the repository rather than authored at one grounding boundary, so it has no single owner. Its HowTo/HowToStep procedure surface is bridged from the kernel because the SUBJECTS are kernel terms, which does not make logic: the catalog owner. Guarding it under logic: would reclassify the pre-existing schema: alignment triples across the domain slices as off-owner residue, each needing a new ceiling: a repo-wide ownership decision, not a ratchet tightening." ;
    gmeow:exemptRowCeiling 7 .
